	"identifying_description": "\"three vultures pattern\"\nColors: brown, blue, red, white, yellow, blue-green\nWarp count: 1-/in.; Warp: Z2S; Weft: Z",
	"web_text": "This early weaving is one of the few extant large-format examples of the parmaklı (“with fingers”) design, commonly referred to as the “three vultures” pattern. The extenuated fingers have been interpreted as the outstretched wings of a bird in flight. The striking quality of this kilim's graphics is achieved by the starkness of its pattern paired with the reductive palette of primary colors and natural brown and white. The pattern is only known to have been woven in three villages, making it especially rare. As with the kilim on its left, this motif has been traced back to the Neolithic period.",
	"didactic_label": "This early weaving is one of the few extant large-format examples of the parmakli (“with fingers”) design, commonly referred to as the “three vultures” pattern. The extenuated fingers have been interpreted as the outstretched wings of a bird in flight. The striking quality of this kilim's graphics is achieved by the starkness of its pattern paired with the reductive palette of primary colors and natural brown and white. The pattern is only known to have been woven in three villages, making it especially rare. As with the kilim on its left, this motif has been traced back to the Neolithic period.",
	"exhibition_history_text": "Anatolian Kilims(SF) 11/90 - 1/91\nSame on tour: Insitut du Monde Arabe, paris 9/91 - 11/91\n Credit Communale, Brussels 12/91 - 3/92\nUnravelling Yarns rotation, (1993-8)\nThe Art of the Anatolian Kilim: Highlights from the McCoy Jones Collection, DY 2011-2",
